# Agent Bridge — what must never enter version control.
#
# This tool's whole job is reporting on other people's repositories, so it holds
# a machine secret and a live picture of where an operator's work lives. Both
# are the sort of thing that reads as harmless until it is pushed.
#
# Runtime state lives OUTSIDE this tree by design -- src/config.mjs resolves
# HOME to AGENTBRIDGE_HOME or ~/.agentbridge -- so the rules below are a belt
# on top of braces. They matter because AGENTBRIDGE_HOME can legitimately be
# pointed at a directory inside a checkout during development, and that is
# exactly when somebody commits their own machine secret by accident.
#
# Versioned here: bridge CODE and lane-policy SCHEMA.
# Never versioned: live session state, credentials, machine identity.

# ── dependencies ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
node_modules/

# ── machine secrets, credentials, runtime state ─────────────────────────────
# .agentbridge/ holds config.json (the sealed-or-plaintext machine secret) and
# registry.json (every registered agent's worktree path -- a map of an
# operator's machine). Both are per-machine and neither is shareable.
.agentbridge/
**/.agentbridge/
config.json
registry.json
*.secret
*.key
*.pem
.env
.env.*

# A DEGRADED init writes the machine secret in PLAINTEXT and says so loudly.
# If that file is ever produced inside a checkout it must not be committable.
*.plaintext
secret.json

# ── heartbeat / session state ───────────────────────────────────────────────
heartbeat*.json
session*.json
state.json
*.state

# ── locks ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Mutex files and the bridge's own lock dirs. A committed lock is a lock nobody
# can clear.
*.lock
locks/
.locks/

# ── logs ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.log
logs/
npm-debug.log*

# ── generated temp / test repositories ──────────────────────────────────────
# Tests create scratch git repos via mkdtemp() in the OS temp dir, so these
# normally never appear here. They are listed because a test run with a
# redirected temp dir would drop them in the working tree, and a committed
# fixture repo is both large and confusing.
ab-*/
tmp/
.tmp/
scratch/
fixtures/generated/

# ── editor / OS noise ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.vscode/
.idea/

# NOTE: package-lock.json is deliberately NOT ignored. It is the lockfile.
supabase/.temp/

# Per-machine Claude Code permission rules. Local by definition: it names
# absolute paths on this machine and encodes what THIS operator has approved.
# Committing it would hand every clone somebody else@s allow-list.
.claude/settings.local.json

# ── agent worktree checkouts ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Claude Code's own EnterWorktree puts checkouts here. Each one holds a `.git`
# FILE pointing back at .git/worktrees/<id>, so committing one is never right --
# git would record it as a gitlink, not the tree.
#
# WITHOUT THIS LINE THE STOP GATE DEADLOCKS. `.claude/` is a PREFIX entry in
# PROTECTED_PATHS (src/guardSession.mjs:47), so an untracked file under it
# refuses baseline minting -- scripts/claude-stop-gate.mjs:228 names that trap
# in a comment: "a stray untracked file there blocks minting forever, which is
# its own outage". The gate offers three remedies, delete / commit / ignore.
# Deleting is refused by our own shell rail (no route removes a directory from
# a session) and committing is wrong per above, so ignoring is the only correct
# one for this path class. Measured 2026-09-17: a stale worktree blocked every
# turn of session 929bee91 until this landed.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, stated so nobody rediscovers it the hard way: the gate can no
# longer see untracked content anywhere under here, and a worktree checkout DOES
# carry a .claude/settings.local.json of its own. The stale one that prompted
# this held "allow": ["Bash(git push:*)"]. So a permission grant can now sit in
# this tree unseen by the baseline check. Danny accepted that trade knowingly
# on 2026-09-17, preferring it to a permanent gate outage. If worktree-carried
# grants ever need watching, that is a job for a check that reads them, not for
# un-ignoring this -- see CLAUDE.md, a check script beats a comment.
.claude/worktrees/
